// SECURITY -- residual gap in the closure/fn-value laundering fix, NOT
// closed this session. Documented here as a standing, NOT-gated repro (same
// status as `tests/mem/struct_arg_leak.kry`'s design-note pattern) so it is
// not silently declared fixed by a future change without re-verification.
//
// The fix closes: a closure/fn-value flowing DIRECTLY through a parameter,
// a `let`-bound local, a return value, a passthrough chain of several call
// layers, an actor message (fire-and-forget send), `spawn`, a generic
// instantiation, and a `dyn Trait` method dispatch -- all verified live
// against the fixed binary, each rejected inside a `deny!` block exactly
// like this file.
//
// NOT closed: a closure/fn-value read back OUT OF A CONTAINER -- a STRUCT
// FIELD (this file), an ARRAY ELEMENT, or a MAP VALUE. `hot_params` only
// tracks parameters/locals whose OWN declared type is `fn(...) -> ...`; a
// parameter typed `Registry` (a struct with a function-typed FIELD), or
// `[fn() -> str]` / `map<str, fn() -> str>`, is never marked hot at all, so
// `resolve_closure_caps` never even attempts to trace the field/element
// value back to its source. This is the SAME root-cause SHAPE the design
// note in `tools/loop/LEDGER.md` flags going in ("a closure stored in a
// struct field and read out -- `registry.reader()` -- is the same hole by a
// different route").
//
// ACCEPTANCE (not yet met): this program should be REJECTED for the same
// reason `cap_escape_closure_launder_deny.kry` now is. Today it compiles
// clean under BOTH `--capabilities-mode=inferred` and `--strict-capabilities`
// and prints the secret from INSIDE a `deny!(fs:read)` block.
//
// Real fix sketch: extend `is_fn_typed`'s notion of "carries a closure" to
// cover a struct type with a function-typed field / an array or map whose
// element type is a function -- mark the PARAMETER hot when the container's
// element/field is invoked (`obj.field()`, `arr[i]()`, `m[k]()`), and extend
// `resolve_closure_caps` to trace a struct/array/map LITERAL's construction
// site (union every value written into the relevant field/element; treat a
// non-literal or a runtime-selected element as `Unknown`, same conservative
// fallback already used for every other unresolvable shape). Comparable in
// size to the parameter-based mechanism just added, not attempted here to
// keep this wave's change reviewable and because the parameter/local/return/
// passthrough/actor/spawn/generic/dyn coverage above is the load-bearing
// majority of idiomatic Kryos closure usage.

struct Registry { reader: fn() -> str }

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn zero_cap_tool(reg: Registry) -> str {
    return reg.reader()
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let r = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let reg = Registry { reader: r }
    deny!(fs:read) {
        println("struct-field launder (NOT CLOSED): " + zero_cap_tool(reg))
    }
}
